1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,857
Good afternoon, everyone.
Today's lecture traces

2
00:00:02,881 --> 00:00:05,567
the history of timekeeping,
from the earliest

3
00:00:05,568 --> 00:00:10,060
sundials right up to the atomic clocks
that keep our modern world running on time.

4
00:00:10,500 --> 00:00:14,000
Let's begin with sundials, which were
developed by the ancient Egyptians.

5
00:00:14,585 --> 00:00:18,840
Their limitation is obvious. They couldn't
be used at night or in cloudy weather.

6
00:00:19,265 --> 00:00:21,663
One early solution was
the water clock, which

7
00:00:21,687 --> 00:00:24,312
measured time as water
dripped out of a container

8
00:00:24,313 --> 00:00:27,312
at a steady rate.
Unfortunately, their accuracy

9
00:00:27,336 --> 00:00:30,232
was affected by
temperature. In cold weather,

10
00:00:30,360 --> 00:00:33,569
the water flowed more
slowly, and it could even freeze.

11
00:00:33,593 --> 00:00:36,067
Hourglasses, filled
with sand, didn't have

12
00:00:36,068 --> 00:00:38,418
that problem, and they
were especially useful on

13
00:00:38,442 --> 00:00:41,526
ships, where sailors used them
to help calculate their speed.

14
00:00:41,527 --> 00:00:46,178
The first mechanical clocks appeared
in Europe in the late 13th century.

15
00:00:46,179 --> 00:00:49,393
These early clocks were large
and expensive, so they were often

16
00:00:49,417 --> 00:00:51,927
placed in church towers,
where the whole community

17
00:00:51,928 --> 00:00:54,488
could see and hear them,
and they were powered

18
00:00:54,512 --> 00:00:57,092
by falling weights. If
you'd looked up at one,

19
00:00:57,200 --> 00:01:00,227
you might have been
surprised by how little it told you.

20
00:01:00,251 --> 00:01:02,172
Early clocks had
only an hour hand,

21
00:01:02,250 --> 00:01:06,620
and they weren't very accurate. They
could gain or lose up to 15 minutes a day.

22
00:01:07,059 --> 00:01:09,497
A truly transformative
moment came when the

23
00:01:09,521 --> 00:01:12,312
Dutch scientist Christian
Huygens built the first

24
00:01:12,324 --> 00:01:15,973
pendulum clock in 1656,
making clocks accurate to

25
00:01:15,997 --> 00:01:19,720
about a minute a day.
However, the motion of ships

26
00:01:19,721 --> 00:01:22,428
made pendulum clocks
unreliable at sea, because the

27
00:01:22,452 --> 00:01:25,012
rolling of the ship threw
off the regular swing.

28
00:01:25,449 --> 00:01:28,621
In the 18th century, John
Harrison solved this problem.

29
00:01:28,645 --> 00:01:30,687
His marine clock
kept accurate time

30
00:01:30,688 --> 00:01:34,032
at sea, which allowed sailors
to calculate their longitude.

31
00:01:34,056 --> 00:01:36,127
On land, meanwhile,
towns originally

32
00:01:36,128 --> 00:01:38,796
set their clocks to noon,
the moment the sun was

33
00:01:38,820 --> 00:01:41,727
highest in their own sky,
so neighbouring towns kept

34
00:01:41,728 --> 00:01:44,948
slightly different times.
The arrival of the railways

35
00:01:44,972 --> 00:01:48,212
made timetables very
confusing, because every station

36
00:01:48,213 --> 00:01:51,992
was working to its own local
time. To solve this, in 1884,

37
00:01:52,016 --> 00:01:55,492
Greenwich in London was
chosen as the prime meridian,

38
00:01:56,004 --> 00:01:59,983
leading to a system of 24 time
zones, which we still use today.

39
00:02:00,007 --> 00:02:02,692
In the 20th century
came the quartz clock.

40
00:02:03,124 --> 00:02:07,480
Quartz crystals vibrate at a very precise
rate when electricity passes through them.

41
00:02:07,929 --> 00:02:11,382
Then, in 1955, the first
practical atomic clock

42
00:02:11,406 --> 00:02:15,080
was built, using cesium
atoms, which vibrate at an

43
00:02:15,081 --> 00:02:18,518
extraordinarily constant
frequency. Today, atomic

44
00:02:18,542 --> 00:02:22,132
clocks carried by satellites
are essential for GPS,

45
00:02:22,295 --> 00:02:26,840
and precise time also underpins the
internet, mobile networks and banking.

46
00:02:26,990 --> 00:02:29,313
That brings us to the
end of today's lecture.

47
00:02:29,337 --> 00:02:31,582
Next week, we'll look
at proposals to reform

48
00:02:31,583 --> 00:02:34,320
how we measure the
length of a second itself.

